\]

The first digit of the dividend is 4, and 4 divided by 2 is 2, so we write 2 on top:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 2 & 1 \\
\cline{2-3}
2 & 3 & \\
\end{array}
\]

Next, we bring down the next digit of the dividend, which is 1:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 2 & 1 \\
\cline{2-3}
2 & 3 & 1 \\
\end{array}
\]

We have 11 in base 5, which is 6 in base 10. 6 divided by 2 is 3, so we write 3 on top:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 3 & \\
\cline{2-3}
2 & 3 & 1 \\
\end{array}
\]

Finally, we bring down the last digit of the dividend, which is 3:

\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & 3 & \\
