 We see that the polynomial $p(x)$ is defined for all real numbers $x$ except $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$

By the Factor Theorem, the roots of $p(x)$ are $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$  Furthermore, $p(a) = p(b) = p(c) = 0,$ so $x - a,$ $x - b,$ and $x - c$ are factors of $p(x).$  Hence,
\begin{align*}
p(x) &= k \left( \frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} \right) \\
&= k \cdot \frac{(x - a)(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)(b - c)}
\end{align*}for some constant $k.$  Since $p(x)$ is a polynomial, the degree of $p(x)$ is the degree of $(x - a)(x - b)(x - c),$ which is $\boxed{3}.$

