We can start by finding the solution to the first congruence, $a\equiv 5\pmod 7$. This implies that $a=5+7b$ for some integer $b$. Substituting this into the second congruence gives
\begin{align*}
5+7b\equiv 10\pmod{11}
\end{align*}
which simplifies to $b\equiv 8\pmod{11}$. Thus, we can set $b=8+11c$ for some integer $c$. Substituting this into our expression for $a$ gives $a=5+7(8+11c)=61+77c=61+7\cdot 11c$, so
\begin{align*}
a\equiv 61\pmod{77}.
\end{align*}
Finally, substituting this into the third congruence gives
\begin{align*}
61+77c\equiv 10\pmod{13}
\end{align*}
which simplifies to $c\equiv 6\pmod{13}$. Thus, we have
\begin{align*}
a=61+77(6+13d)=559+961d=559+9\cdot 106d=559+93\cdot 2d,
\end{align*}
so $a\equiv 559\pmod{93}$. Since $a\in[0,93)$, the smallest positive solution to this congruence is $a=70$, but this is not in the interval $0\leq a<1001$. Therefore, the solution is $a=70+93=163$, and the largest integer less than $2010$ that satisfies all three congruences is $1001\cdot 2+163=\boxed{2165}$.

