 We look for integer roots of $x^4 + 5x^3 + 9x^2 - x - 14 = 0.$
By the Integer Root Theorem, any integer root $p$ must divide $14,$
so the possible values of $p$ are $\pm 1,$ $\pm 2,$ $\pm 7,$ and $\pm 14.$

We can check that $x = -7$ is a root:
\begin{align*}
(-7)^4 + 5(-7)^3 + 9(-7)^2 - (-7) - 14 &= 2401 - 8575 + 441 + 7 - 14 \\
&= 0.
\end{align*}Therefore, by the Factor Theorem, $x + 7$ is a factor of the polynomial.

We can perform polynomial division to divide $x^4 + 5x^3 + 9x^2 - x - 14$ by $x + 7$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc cc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^3} & +2x^2 &+5x&-2  \\
\cline{2-6}
x+7 & x^4 &+ 5x^3 &+ 9x^2&-x&-14  \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^4} & +7x^3  \\ 
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 2x^3 & +9x^2  \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 2x^3 & +14x^2  \\ 
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} &  & -5x^2 & -x  \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} &  & -5x^2 & -35x  \\ 
\cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} &  &  & 34x & -14  \\
\end{array}
\]This tells us that
\[x^4 + 5x^3 + 9x^2 - x - 14 = (x + 7)(x^3 + 2x^2 + 5x - 2).\]We can then check the possible integer values of $x$ that satisfy $x^3 + 2x^2 + 5x - 2 = 0$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc c}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & +5x & +2  \\
\cline{2-5}
x+2 & x^3 &+ 2x^2 &+5x&-2  \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^3} & +2x^2  \\ 
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 5x^2 & +5x  \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 5x^2 & +10x  \\ 
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} &  & -5x & -2  \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} &  & -5x & -10  \\ 
\cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} &  &  & 8   \\
\end{array}
\]Since $8 = 2^3,$ and we have already found the integer root $x = -7,$
the only other possible integer root is $x = -2.$

We can check that $x = -2$ is not a root:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc c}
