 The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ is given by the formula
\[\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.\]So, we want the vectors $\mathbf{v}$ such that
\[\frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.\]If $\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = 0,$ then the set of all vectors $\mathbf{v}$ is the line orthogonal to $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.$

[asy]
import geometry;

unitsize(0.8 cm);

pair P = (2,1);
pair Q = (5,-2);
pair R = (0,0);

draw((-1,0)--(6,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,3));
draw((0,0)--P,Arrow(6));
draw(P--Q,dashed,Arrow(6));
draw((0,0)--Q,Arrow(6));
draw(R--Q,dashed);

label("$\mathbf{v}$", Q, SE);
label("$\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$", P, E);
[/asy]

Note that $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = 5,$ so $\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ if and only if
\[\mathbf{v} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = 2x + y = 0.\]Thus, the equation of the line is $\boxed{y = -2x}.$

 Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair P, Q;

P = (2,1);
Q = (0,0);

draw((-2,0)--(3,0));
draw((0,-2)--(0,3));
draw((0,0)--P,Arrow(6));
draw((0,0)--Q,Arrow(6));
draw(P--Q,dashed,Arrow(6));

dot("$\mathbf{v}$", (1.5,2), NE);
[/asy]

Then
\[\operatorname{proj}_{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\|^2} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{2x + y}{5} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 4x/5 + 2y/5 \\ 2x/5 + y/5 \end{pmatrix}.\]Thus, we want
\begin{align*}
4x/5 + 2y/5 &= 0, \\
2x/5 + y/5 &= 0.
\end{align*}Subtracting these equations, we get $3x/5 = y/5,$ so $y = 3x.$  Thus, the equation of the line is $\boxed{y = 3x}.$

 Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}.$

